    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d370">

                <pb facs="d369-3" n="3" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v03mSupp/d369-3"/>

                <head>370. Memorandum of Conversation<anchor n="1" xml:id="d370-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>New York</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" type="conversation-or-meeting-date"
                            when="1958-08-20T21:45:00-04:00">August 20, 1958, 9:45
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <pb facs="d370-1" n="1" type="facsimile" xml:id="d370-1"/>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>PARTICIPANTS</label>
                    <item><list>
                            <item><hi rendition="#italic">United States</hi></item>
                            <item>The Secretary</item>
                            <item>Mr. Reinhardt</item>
                            <item>Mr. Greene</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <item><list>
                            <item><hi rendition="#italic">United Kingdom</hi></item>
                            <item>Mr. Lloyd</item>
                            <item>Sir William Hayter</item>
                            <item>Mr. A. R. Moore</item>
                            <item>Mr. Dennis Lasky</item>
                        </list></item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item><hi rendition="#italic">Statement on Suspension of Nuclear
                        Testing</hi></item>
                </list>
                <p>The Secretary showed Mr. Lloyd the draft
                    of a statement proposed to be made by the President on suspension of nuclear
                    testing. In a lengthy discussion Mr. Lloyd argued that unless there be established in the statement a
                    relationship between our readiness to suspend testing and Soviet willingness to
                    join in negotiations for an agreement to supervise suspension of testing, we
                    would give away to the Soviets <pb facs="1537" n="1432" type="typeset"
                        xml:id="pg1432"/> without exacting any price an essential indeed fundamental
                    part of our position.</p>
                <p>As a result of this discussion, the Secretary agreed to insert at the beginning
                    of the fourth paragraph the phrase “If this is accepted in principle”, so as to
                    establish the link between the two parts of our statement.</p>
            </div>
